# Native process memory: Fai / Rust / OCaml

**Snapshot:** `fixture-checkpoint` · **Completed:** 2026-10-12T00:00:01Z

**Measured main revision:** [`aaaaaaaaaaaa`](https://github.com/example/fai/tree/aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa) · **Method:** `isolated-memory-v3`

**Host:** fixture / fixture · **CPU:** fixture

**Peers:** fixture · OCaml fixture (Flambda, `-O3`)

[Raw data, identities and reproducibility record](https://example.test/evidence)

[Matched kernels](#matched-kernels) · [Application comparisons](#application-comparisons)

## Current coverage

**50 workloads · 254 size/repetition cases · 5 fresh processes per side and mode.** Predeclared memory-specific input curves and 1/16 whole-workload repetitions are included. Additional storage fixtures vary elements, keys, nodes, UTF-8 chunks and body counts. Every peer is an isolated, uninstrumented executable. Input units and fixed-state cases are listed below.

The tables report **process footprint**, including code, stacks, allocator retention and GC policy. Each displayed peak and floor is the maximum across fresh processes, in KiB (1,024 bytes). They do not measure comparable cross-language live heaps. Floors use the same executable; peaks are never subtracted or converted into compiler-memory win counts.

## Comparison and floor legend

The floor is the same executable running its harness without the workload. `Process F/R` is Fai's peak RSS divided by Rust's; `Process F/O` divides it by OCaml's. Lower ratios mean a smaller observed process footprint.

| Label | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `matched kernel` | Peers share the reviewed algorithm and logical data structures. Runtime representation and allocator costs remain observable. |
| `application` | Algorithms, containers or implementation policies differ. Ratios still compare total process footprints, including those choices. |
| `Floor share F/R/O` | Each language's floor divided by its workload peak, shown as a percentage. A high share describes a baseline-dominated process; it does not suppress ratios. Independent process observations can give a share above 100%. |
| `F/R: …` or `F/O: …` | Only the named ratio is withheld because its pair is missing, incompatible, instrumented, or has fewer than five observations. |

`—` denotes an unavailable value or withheld ratio, with the reason shown alongside the comparison. Floors are never subtracted. Whole-process RSS includes code, stacks, allocator retention and GC policy; live-heap efficiency and Perceus guarantees require their own evidence.

## Matched kernels

Peers share the reviewed algorithm, logical data and evaluation phases. Native representation, allocation and reclamation costs remain observable.

**41 workloads · 208 size/repetition cases.** Statistics and tables below cover this category only.

### Process-RSS ratio summary

Ratios are **Fai ÷ peer; lower means a smaller process footprint**. Arithmetic and geometric means use unrounded case ratios with equal weight per workload/input/repetition case. P90 is nearest-rank across case ratios, not a percentile of RSS samples. Only compatible, uninstrumented pairs with sufficient observations contribute; large floor shares remain included.

| Peer | Paired / cases | Geometric mean | Arithmetic mean | P90 |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| rust | 208 / 208 | 1.000× | 1.000× | 1.000× |
| ocaml | 208 / 208 | 1.000× | 1.000× | 1.000× |
